= Barley

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Hordeum vulgare_ L., family Poaceae; the malted/sprouted grain is _Hordei fructus germinatus_ in modern pharmacognosy. Classical Latin _hordeum_ "barley," from a PIE root \*_ǵʰers-_ / \*_ǵʰrzd-_ "to bristle, stand stiff" — i.e. "the bristly [grain]," named for its long awns. Cognate with Greek κριθή (_krithḗ_, "barley"). The Roman attitude is worth noting: barley was treated as inferior to wheat, fit for animals and given to disgraced legionaries (_hordearii_, "barley-eaters") as a punishment ration.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _hordeum_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _hordeum_). The Greek κριθή and the PIE bristle-root: Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_; Watkins, _Indo-European Roots_ (\*_ǵʰers-_). On the Roman _hordearii_ punishment ration: standard classical references on the Roman military diet (e.g. Roth, _The Logistics of the Roman Army at War_).]

*English:* _Barley_, from Old English _bærlic_ (adj., "of barley"), from _bere_ "barley" (still preserved in Scots _bere_ and in _barn_ < OE _bere-ærn_ "barley-house"). _Bere_ derives from Proto-Germanic \*_bariz-_ / \*_baraz-_, from PIE \*_bʰars-_ "spike, bristle, beard," whence also Latin _far_ (emmer/spelt) and English _beard_ — so barley, beard, and the older Latin grain-word share one root: the bristle/awn.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "barley, n.", "bere, n.", "barn, n."); Watkins, _Indo-European Roots_ (\*_bʰars-_ "spike, bristle"). The OE _bere-ærn_ → _barn_ etymology is standard.]

*Chinese:* 大麥 (dàmài), "barley" — lit. "large mài" (大 dà = large; 麥 mài = the wheat/barley grain-graph), explicit contrast with 小麥 (xiǎomài, wheat, "small mài"). The sprouted/malted grain is treated as a *distinct drug*: 麥芽 (màiyá, "wheat-sprout") — Hordei Fructus Germinatus — sometimes 大麥芽 (dàmàiyá) to disambiguate from millet- and wheat-sprouts. 大麥茶 (dàmài chá), roasted-barley tea, is a longstanding East Asian dietetic preparation.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典). 大麥 / 麥芽 as distinct drugs: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004); Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, grain section.]

*Sanskrit:* यव (_yava_) — one of the oldest attested grain-names in the Indo-Aryan record, present from the Ṛgveda; cognate with Greek ζειά (_zeiá_, "emmer/spelt") and Avestan _yauua-_, from PIE \*_yewo-_ "grain, cereal." In modern Hindi, _jau_ (जौ). Distinct named forms in the classical literature: green/unripe barley (_haritayava_) and the various preparations — _saktu_ (roasted barley flour), _yavāgū_ (gruel), _mantha_ (barley-and-water draught) — are treated as having their own dietetic profiles. Note that in some classical passages _yava_ is used as a generic "grain" or for the size-unit (_yava_ = a barleycorn, a standard small measure), so context matters.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _yava_, _haritayava_, _saktu_, _yavāgū_, _mantha_); Mayrhofer, _Etymologisches Wörterbuch des Altindoarischen_ (s.v. _yáva-_) for the PIE \*_yewo-_ etymology and Greek/Avestan cognates. The _yava_ measure: standard Sanskrit metrology — see e.g. notes in the Sharangadhara Saṃhitā.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Barley appears in the materia medica chiefly under two related but distinct drug names — the grain itself (大麥) and the sprouted/malted grain (麥芽) — the latter being by far the more commonly prescribed.#footnote[大麥 / 麥芽 as distinct entries: _Bencao Gangmu_, grain section; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The clinical centre of gravity is firmly on 麥芽.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* 大麥 — cool (涼) to slightly cool; 麥芽 — neutral (平), some sources mark slightly warm when dry-fried (炒麥芽).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* 大麥 — sweet (甘 gān), with a slight salty note in some sources; 麥芽 — sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* 大麥 — Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃). 麥芽 — Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Liver (肝).#footnote[Property, flavor, channel data: _Bencao Gangmu_ 大麥 / 麥芽 entries; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- *大麥* — 和胃寬腸 / 益氣調中 (harmonizes the Stomach, eases the intestines, augments qi and regulates the middle); 消渴 (relieves wasting-and-thirst, _xiāokě_); diuretic effect (利小便). Dietetically used as a cooling, easy-to-digest grain — the basis of 大麥茶 / 大麥粥.
- *麥芽* — *消食和中* (the signature action: disperses food stagnation and harmonizes the middle), with a particular reputation for digesting *starchy/grain stagnation* (米麵食積) — explicit complement to 山楂 (hawthorn) for meat-fat stagnation and 神麯 (medicated leaven) for general food stagnation.
- *麥芽 — 回乳消脹* (a high dose, customarily 30–120 g, *stops lactation* and reduces breast distension; a low dose, ~10–15 g, has been reported to *promote* lactation — a dose-dependent biphasic reputation worth flagging as classically noted).
- *麥芽 — 疏肝 / 舒肝* (soothes constrained Liver qi, especially Liver qi affecting the Stomach — gives it a small but real role alongside the standard Liver-soothing herbs).#footnote[Action lists: _Bencao Gangmu_ 大麥 / 麥芽 entries; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The biphasic dose–lactation reputation is classical and is preserved in modern Chinese pharmacology textbooks (e.g. _Zhong Yao Xue_, People's Medical Publishing House); a real mechanism is unsettled — flag as classical observation, not modern-pharmacology established.]

*Cautions:* 麥芽's lactation-suppressing action makes it inappropriate during lactation when continued nursing is intended. The biphasic dose effect is a recurring source of clinical confusion — note dose explicitly. 大麥, as a cooling grain, is dietetically cautioned in Spleen-yang vacuity with cold patterns.#footnote[Lactation caution: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; standard modern Chinese pharmacology texts. The dose-direction reputation: classically recorded, mechanism not modern-established — flagged.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Yava (यव) is a classical and emblematic _dravya_, repeatedly singled out in Caraka (Sūtrasthāna 27) and treated as the paradigmatic _laghu_ ("light") cultivated grain — the canonical counter-pole to godhūma (wheat) within the _śūka-dhānya_ group of awned grains.#footnote[Yava in the _śūka-dhānya_ group, with extensive dedicated verses: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27.13–17 (the yava passage), and Sūtrasthāna 5 (dietary regimen); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 6; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba).]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) with Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent) — the astringent note is the explicit point of contrast with wheat's plain madhura.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry); also _sara_ (mobile, mildly laxative) and _picchila_-rahita (non-slimy). The set is the precise inverse of wheat's _guru-snigdha-sāndra_.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent) — the key divergence from wheat (madhura vipāka). This is the dravyaguna basis for yava's _lekhana_ (scraping/reducing) and _medohara_ (fat-reducing) reputation: kaṭu vipāka tends to dry, divide, and reduce.#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of yava: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27.13–17; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 6 (Murthy translation, Chaukhamba). The wheat ↔ barley contrast (sweet vs. pungent vipāka; guru-snigdha vs. laghu-rūkṣa) is a textbook classical opposition.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No clearly distinct prabhāva is assigned to yava in the core saṃhitās — its _medohara_, _lekhana_, _kledahara_ ("damp-removing"), and _mūtrala_ ("diuretic") reputation is treated as flowing from kaṣāya-madhura rasa, laghu-rūkṣa guṇa, śīta vīrya, and especially kaṭu vipāka. Later commentarial and modern dravyaguna writers sometimes promote yava's _medohara_ action to a prabhāva-level reading; the canon does not require this, and the elevation is flagged as interpretive.#footnote[On prabhāva as the residual category beyond rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The interpretive promotion of yava's _medohara_ to prabhāva-level appears in some modern dravyaguna textbooks (e.g. P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-Vijñāna_) — flagged as commentarial/modern.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha and Pitta (kapha-pitta-śāmaka); may aggravate Vāta if taken alone, dry, or in excess — hence the classical pairing of yava with ghee, milk, or honey-water in regimens for Vāta-sensitive constitutions.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Lekhana_ — scraping, reducing (the defining action: reduces _meda_/fat and _kleda_/excess moisture).
- _Medohara_ — fat-reducing; the prescribed grain in _sthaulya_ (obesity) and _prameha_ (the umbrella category for diabetes-type disorders) regimens.
- _Mūtrala_ — diuretic; explicitly cited in the saṃhitās for _mūtra-kṛcchra_ (dysuria) and _aśmarī_ (urinary calculus) regimens, often as _yavakṣāra_ (barley-ash alkali) or barley decoction.
- _Sthairyakara_ — stabilizing, gives steadiness (Caraka's specific term for yava in Sū. 27).
- _Balya_ — strength-giving (despite being _laghu_ — Caraka's grouping of yava with rice and wheat among the _balya_ grains for fit, active people).
- _Kledahara_ — reduces excess body moisture/dampness.
- _Sara_ — mildly laxative.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27.13–17 (the yava verses, including the _sthairyakara_/_balya_ designations); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; Suśruta, Sūtrasthāna 46. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba); Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_. _Yavakṣāra_ as a recognized alkaline preparation: _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional; M = modern/medical)_

- *(S) Conditional (avasthā / doṣa) viruddha:* yava's _rūkṣa_ (drying) and kaṭu vipāka render it inappropriate in pure Vāta excess, _karśya_ (emaciation), _kṣata-kṣīṇa_ (wasting from chest injury), convalescence requiring _bṛṃhaṇa_, and Vāta-dominant constipation when taken plain — the saṃhitā remedy is to take yava cooked with ghee/milk (e.g. _yavāgū_, _mantha_).
- *(N) Combination cautions:* eating roasted barley flour (_saktu_) dry, at night, in excess, or stirred with the left hand is explicitly cautioned in classical _saktu_-rules (Caraka, Sūtrasthāna 5) — a famous worked example of the _saṃskāra_ / _vidhi_ branch of viruddha, in which proper preparation and timing matter as much as the substance itself.
- *(N/F) Combination cautions:* yava with curd/_dadhi_ in heavy quantity is cautioned as compounding kapha-aggravating effects in susceptible constitutions; specific saṃyoga rules vary by source.
- *(M) Modern medical — _de facto_:* barley contains the gluten-related prolamin *hordein* and is contraindicated in *celiac disease* and *non-celiac gluten sensitivity*; barley is also a major source of fermentable fructans (FODMAPs). These are modern medical contraindications, not classical viruddha — flagged.#footnote[_Saktu_-vidhi rules and the _yavāgū_/_mantha_ corrective preparations: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 5 and 27 — a canonical worked example of _saṃskāra_-class viruddha. (M) celiac/NCGS/FODMAP cautions: modern gastroenterology — verify against current guidelines (e.g. ACG Clinical Guideline on celiac disease).]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *麥芽 in food-stagnation formulas* — combined with 山楂 (hawthorn, for meat/fat stagnation), 神麯 (medicated leaven, for general food stagnation), and 萊菔子 (radish seed) as the standard quartet for _shíjī_ (food accumulation). The classical pairing 焦三仙 (jiāo sānxiān, "three immortals charred") = charred 麥芽 + charred 山楂 + charred 神麯 is a ubiquitous food-stagnation dispersing combination.
- *麥芽 for Liver-Stomach disharmony* — small to moderate doses in formulas addressing Liver qi constraint affecting digestion; pairs with 柴胡 (bupleurum) and 香附 (cyperus).
- *麥芽 for lactation suppression* — used at *high dose* (30–120 g) when weaning is desired; this use is well documented from the Song dynasty onwards.
- *大麥茶 / 大麥粥* — roasted-barley tea and barley congee as dietetic preparations: cool, fluid-generating, easy on the Spleen-Stomach; widely used in Chinese, Korean (보리차 _boricha_), and Japanese (麦茶 _mugicha_) culinary tradition.#footnote[麥芽 combinations and 焦三仙: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009); Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The Song-dynasty lactation-suppression use is traced in modern materia medica historiography. Barley tea/congee dietetics: Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Yavāgū (gruel) and yavānna* — barley gruels and barley-meals as staple convalescent and reducing diets; varieties of yavāgū with specific medicaments (e.g. _ṣaḍ-yavāgū_) are catalogued at length in Caraka, Sūtrasthāna 2 and elsewhere — a whole class of _peyā_ / gruel preparations is built around yava.
- *Saktu (roasted barley flour)* — a paradigmatic Ayurvedic food-medicine; eaten as a draught with water, ghee, sugar, or buttermilk (the _mantha_ preparation). Caraka's _saktu-vidhi_ (Sū. 5) is the locus classicus for _saṃskāra_-class viruddha rules.
- *Yava in sthaulya / prameha / medoroga regimens* — yava (often as roti or saktu) is *the* prescribed grain in classical Ayurvedic regimens for obesity (_sthaulya_), prameha (diabetes-type disorders), and _medoroga_ (lipid-disorders), on the strength of its _lekhana_ / _medohara_ action — the great classical counterpoise to wheat's _bṛṃhaṇa_.
- *Yavakṣāra* (यवक्षार, "barley-ash alkali") — the alkaline ash of burnt barley plants; a recognized _kṣāra_ preparation used internally for digestive stagnation, _gulma_, and urinary stone (_aśmarī_); listed in the _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_.
- *Yavāgvāgnimantha-cūrṇa, Yavāni-derived preparations* — multiple yava-based formulary entries; barley-derived alkalis (with _ajamodā_, etc.) are common excipients in compound powders.#footnote[Yavāgū types and the _peyā_ class: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 2; saktu-vidhi: Caraka, Sūtrasthāna 5. Yava in _sthaulya_/_prameha_: Caraka, Sūtrasthāna 21 (_aṣṭauninditīya_, the eight despised constitutions, including the obese) and Cikitsāsthāna 6 (_prameha-cikitsā_); the _yava-roṭikā_ regimen is a classical commonplace. Yavakṣāra: _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, vol. I.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Hippocratic ptisanē* (πτισάνη) — barley gruel/decoction, the staple sick-room food of the Hippocratic corpus; the treatise _On Regimen in Acute Diseases_ is largely a treatise on the rational use of ptisanē. _Tisane_ in modern French and English descends directly from this term.
- *Dioscorides and Galen* — both treat barley (κριθή, _hordeum_) as cooling and drying, less nourishing than wheat but more cleansing; Galen treats it as cooling-and-drying in the humoral schema. Galenic preparations include _alphita_ (barley meal, used in poultices), barley-water as a demulcent, and various barley-based emollient cataplasms.
- *Roman dietary culture* — wheat for citizens, barley for animals and as punishment rations for disgraced soldiers; gladiators were famously called _hordearii_ ("barley-men") for their barley-heavy training diet.
- *Mediaeval and early modern* — pearl barley (_hordeum perlatum_) as a demulcent and convalescent food; barley water as a sick-room standard; in the British pharmacopoeial tradition, _decoctum hordei_ (barley decoction) persists into the 19th-century pharmacopoeias.
- *Modern survivals* — pearl/pot barley as a kitchen staple; barley water (lemon barley water) as a folk demulcent; malted barley as the foundation of beer, whisky, and malt extract (_extractum malti_), the last of which entered modern pharmacy as a nutritive and as a vehicle for cod-liver oil.#footnote[Hippocratic ptisanē: _On Regimen in Acute Diseases_ (Loeb edition, W. H. S. Jones); the etymological line ptisanē → tisane is standard. Dioscorides on κριθή / ἄλφιτα: _De Materia Medica_ 2.86 (Beck translation); Galen, _De alimentorum facultatibus_ 1.11 on barley (Powell translation, Cambridge). Gladiators as _hordearii_: Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ 18.72; cf. Roth, _The Logistics of the Roman Army at War_. Pearl barley / _decoctum hordei_ / _extractum malti_: Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_ — verify pharmacopoeial details against current sources.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Barley is fully canonical in Tibb and is one of the most-prominent Prophetic-medicine substances — the basis of the_ talbīna _hadith. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* شعير (_shaʿīr_). Persian: جو (_jow_). Urdu: جو (_jow_) and شعير (_shaʿīr_).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* the *talbīna (تلبينة)* hadith — *the foundational Prophetic-medicine barley reference*: _"Talbīna is comforting for the heart of the patient and dispels some of the sadness"_ (Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Aṭ'imah_; Muslim, _Kitāb al-Salām_). *Talbīna* is a thin, milky barley-flour broth (the name from _laban_, "milk," because of its milk-like appearance); recommended for the sick, the bereaved, and during convalescence. The hadith is universally cited and *talbīna* is widely prepared in modern Islamic households as a Prophetic-medicine practice. The Prophet's preparation (_sawīq_, barley meal) is also widely attested.

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _shaʿīr_; Galen had given the same). The Cold-Dry classification *converges with the Ayurvedic śīta-rūkṣa profile* — a clean cross-system convergence; the Greco-Arabic and the Indian traditions agree on barley's cooling-drying character.

*Af'āl:* *Mubarriḍ* (cooling); *mughadhdhī yasīr* (mildly nourishing); *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating — the talbīna register); *mufattiḥ* (mild deobstruent); *mudirr li-l-bawl* (diuretic); *munaffith* (expectorant for hot-pattern cough); *ḥāfiẓ li-l-qulūb* (heart-comforting).

*Istemālāt:* hot-pattern fever (the canonical Tibb fever-food, paralleling the Hippocratic ptisane); summer-heat; convalescence; thirst (*ʿaṭash*); *amrāḍ ṣafrāwiyya* (bilious diseases); melancholic depression (the talbīna indication); dryness; dyspepsia of hot pattern; jaundice.

*Mawāne':* heavy phlegmatic constitution; cold-pattern diarrhea; severe abdominal cold; celiac disease (modern, absolute).

*Muṣliḥ:* honey or sugar; warming spices (cinnamon, cardamom) for phlegmatic individuals; ghee for the nourishing form.

*Badal:* wheat (*qamḥ*) for general nourishment; oats (*shūfān*) in more modern texts.

*Tibb formulas:* *Talbīna (تلبينة)* — the canonical Prophetic-medicine preparation; *Mā' al-Shaʿīr (ماء الشعير)* — barley water, the classical fever / sick-room drink (the lineage from Hippocratic ptisane → Galenic decoctum hordei → Arabic mā' al-shaʿīr → modern British "barley water" is unbroken); *Sawīq al-Shaʿīr* — toasted barley meal in water; *Joshānda-yi Shaʿīr* — barley decoction for hot-pattern fever; *Sharbat-i Shaʿīr* — barley syrup.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _shaʿīr_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _shaʿīr_; Bukhari _Kitāb al-Aṭ'imah_ and Muslim _Kitāb al-Salām_ for talbīna; Ibn Qayyim, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_, talbīna chapter; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _jow / shaʿīr_ entry.#footnote[Tibb shaʿīr: classical Galenic-Avicennan reading, with Prophetic-medicine elevation via the talbīna hadith. The cross-system convergence with Ayurveda is exact (both Cold-Dry / śīta-rūkṣa); divergence with the Ayurvedic _kapha-hara_ being more central in barley's Indian register than in the Tibb register.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Barley is cultivated across an exceptionally wide climatic band — from the warm-dry Mediterranean and Fertile Crescent of its domestication, across cold-dry Central Asian and Tibetan highlands, to the cool-damp British Atlantic fringe. Its dietetic role varies precisely with that climate, and the canonical preparations of each zone read as climatic-constitutional *corrections* of what that climate does to the body. The reading below applies the standard classical climatic logic: in dravyaguna, cold-dry climates aggravate Vāta, hot-dry climates aggravate Pitta, cool-damp climates aggravate Kapha (with Vāta in winter); in 中醫, the six external excesses (六淫 — 風寒暑濕燥火) attach to climates and to organs (cold→Kidney, damp→Spleen, dryness→Lung).#footnote[Climatic-constitutional dietetics, classical foundations: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 6 (_ṛtucaryā_, seasonal regimen); Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 3 (_ṛtucaryā_); _Huang Di Nei Jing_ (黃帝內經), _Su Wen_ chs. 2 (_si qi tiao shen da lun_ 四氣調神大論) and 67–74 (the 五運六氣 doctrine of climatic-pathogenic correspondences). Treating regional staple preparations as climatic correctives is implicit throughout Caraka's _annapānavidhi_ (Sū. 27) and made explicit in later regional dietetic literature.]

=== Mediterranean & Middle Eastern (warm-dry, Pitta-aggravating)

Hot, dry, sunny — the Fertile-Crescent climate of barley's domestication. Constitutional tendency: Pitta excess, _rasakṣaya_ (depletion of fluids), and (in 中醫 terms) external 燥 (dryness) injuring fluids; Liver-Yang rises easily. Barley's śīta vīrya and madhura-kaṣāya rasa make it a natural corrective.

Preparation: Hippocratic *ptisanē* (long-decocted barley gruel, sometimes with vinegar, honey, or lemon); Levantine *kashk* (fermented barley with milk solids); *jureesh* (cracked-barley pottage); barley water with lemon; in North Africa, sweetened barley gruels and barley couscous.

Cares: classical preparations *retain moisture* — long decoction, dairy or yogurt accompaniment, lemon — and are constitutively Pitta- and dryness-pacifying. Dry-roasted preparations alone would not suit this climate.#footnote[Hippocratic ptisanē: _On Regimen in Acute Diseases_ (Loeb, Jones). Levantine kashk and jureesh as preparations: standard regional ethnographies of Middle Eastern food (e.g. Roden, _A Book of Middle Eastern Food_) — not classical-medical sources, but reliable on preparation. The 燥 doctrine and Lung-correspondence: _Su Wen_ chs. 5 and 71.]

=== Tibetan / Himalayan / Central Asian (cold-dry, high-altitude — extreme Vāta)

Cold-dry-windy at altitude: the canonical Vāta climate; in 中醫, chronic exposure to external 寒 (cold) and 風 (wind), with sustained Kidney-yang load. *Naked / hulless barley* (_Hordeum vulgare_ var. _nudum_; Tibetan _ne_ ནས་) is the staple because no other cereal thrives at the altitude — a clean case of climate selecting the grain.

Preparation: *tsampa* (རྩམ་པ་) — barley dry-roasted then ground, kneaded with *po cha* (yak-butter tea: brick tea + yak butter + salt). The triad is a textbook climatic correction: roasting (अग्निसंस्कार _agnisaṃskāra_) lightens digestion and gives a warming residue; butter is _snigdha_ at the level a plateau body needs; salt supports Kidney-yang against cold; tea moves stagnation. The Tibetan medical canon (_rGyud bzhi_) treats roasted barley + butter + salt + tea as a constitutionally complete staple — itself a worked example of the dravyaguna-vidhi rule that a grain's appropriate preparation is climate-dependent.

Cares: barley *alone* in this climate would aggravate Vāta — the butter, salt, and tea are constitutive, not optional. Raw or cold-soaked preparations are inappropriate. The roasting step is essential, both for shelf life and for digestive ease (raw barley flour ferments).#footnote[_rGyud bzhi_ (རྒྱུད་བཞི་), the Four Tantras attributed to Yutok Yontan Gonpo (12th c.), treats roasted barley centrally in its dietetic chapters — verify against a modern critical edition (e.g. Clark, _The Quintessence Tantras of Tibetan Medicine_). Tsampa preparation and po cha composition: standard Tibetan ethnographies and Tibetan-medical secondary literature (e.g. Meyer, _Gso-ba Rig-pa_). The _agnisaṃskāra_ category — heat-transformation as a grade of _saṃskāra_-class correction — is from Caraka Sū. 26.]

=== Cool-damp North European (Scotland, Ireland, Atlantic fringe — Vāta-Kapha)

Cool, damp, windy, low-sun: a Vāta-Kapha climate in dravyaguna; in 中醫, predisposing to external 寒濕 (cold-damp) invasion and Spleen-yang weakness.

Preparation: *Scotch broth* (barley + mutton + root vegetables) — slow-simmered, Spleen-strengthening; *barley bannocks* and *bere-meal scones* (Orkney's _bere_, the old four-row barley); fermented barley as *beer*, *ale*, *whisky*. The fermentation route deserves note as a _saṃskāra_ inversion: malting + fermentation turns a cooling-laghu grain into a warming-vīrya finished product, paralleling how curd warms milk in Ayurvedic dravyaguna.

Cares: cold/raw barley preparations are climatically inappropriate; long cooking and accompaniment with warming, drying _kapha-haka_ supports (onion, leek, mutton, root vegetables) is the classical pattern. Beer and whisky, in moderation, fit the _saṃskāra_-logic; excess shifts into modern alcohol-toxicology — flagged.#footnote[Orkney _bere_ as a surviving four-row landrace: modern Scottish agricultural histories and the Scottish Agricultural Bere Project literature. The _saṃskāra_ inversion principle (cooling raw → warming fermented): standard dravyaguna logic, with _saṃskāra_ as a correction class in Caraka Sū. 26 and as a transformation principle in Bhāvaprakāśa _kṛtānna-varga_.]

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

Modern globalized eating routinely moves a preparation across climates without adjusting it — Mediterranean cold lemon-barley salad in Glasgow in February, hot tsampa in the tropics. Classically these are *non-corrective* preparations: not toxic, but no longer doing the climatic-constitutional work for which the original form was selected — a useful pedagogical category, _deśa-viruddha_ (place-incompatible), worth naming explicitly.#footnote[_Deśa-viruddha_ as a recognized conditional class within viruddha: Caraka Sū. 26. The category covers food-place mismatches; its modern application to globalized eating is interpretive but tracks the classical principle.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Barley is a staple food and (as malt) a major industrial substrate; figures are approximate, for whole-grain hulled barley per 100 g unless noted. *Verify against USDA FoodData Central*, current edition; values vary substantially among hulled, hulless ("naked"), pearled, and malted forms.#footnote[Composition figures are approximate; check against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition. Hulled, hulless, pearled, and malted barley differ in fiber, β-glucan, and mineral profile.]

*Approximate composition (whole-grain hulled barley, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~354 kcal; Water ~10 g
- Protein ~12 g (the gluten-related prolamin is *hordein*; barley is *not* gluten-free)
- Carbohydrate ~73 g, predominantly *starch*; fiber ~17 g — of which a notably high fraction is *β-glucan*, the soluble fiber responsible for barley's modern pharmacological reputation
- Fat ~2.3 g
- Minerals: magnesium ~133 mg, phosphorus ~264 mg, potassium ~452 mg, iron ~3.6 mg, zinc ~2.8 mg, *manganese* ~1.9 mg (very high), selenium ~38 μg
- B-vitamins: thiamine, niacin, B6, folate; vitamin E in the germ
- *Phytate* in the bran reduces mineral absorption; pearling/sprouting/soaking attenuates this

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *β-glucan* — the soluble-fiber star of the modern barley story; barley β-glucan reliably *lowers LDL cholesterol* in a dose-dependent way at ~3 g/day intake, and *blunts postprandial glycemia*. The FDA and EFSA both recognize qualified health claims on this basis.
- *Glycemic response* — whole, intact, or hulless barley has a notably low glycemic index; pearled barley is moderate; malt syrup is high. The Ayurvedic _medohara_ / TCM dietetic reading lines up well here.
- *Hordein and gluten cross-reactivity* — barley is *unsafe in celiac disease* (alongside wheat and rye); "gluten-free" labels exclude barley and barley-malt.
- *Sprouted barley (麥芽) and amylase* — sprouting activates α- and β-amylase, the basis of malting and a plausible mechanism for the classical _xiāoshí_ (food-digesting) action on starch-stagnation. Modern pharmacological characterization remains modest — flagged.
- *Fructans (FODMAPs)* — barley is a significant fructan source; relevant in IBS-type sensitivities.
- *Selenium and manganese* — barley is a meaningful dietary source of both.

*Safe daily intake / limits:* No toxicological upper limit for barley as a staple food in the general population. The operative limits are conditional:
- *Absolute avoidance* in celiac disease and barley/wheat-cross-allergy.
- *Restriction* in FODMAP intolerance, per individual tolerance.
- Cholesterol-lowering and glycemic-blunting effects are seen from roughly *3 g β-glucan/day*, achievable from ~40–60 g whole/hulless barley (variety-dependent); this is a *therapeutic-target* figure, not a safety threshold.
- *麥芽 (sprouted barley) clinical dosing* — 9–15 g for digestive use; 30–120 g for lactation suppression — these are *medicinal*, not dietary, doses; the lactation-suppressing dose is *contraindicated during desired lactation*. Reference figures — verify against current Chinese pharmacopoeia and clinician judgement.#footnote[β-glucan / LDL cholesterol: EFSA, _Scientific Opinion on the substantiation of health claims related to beta-glucans from oats and barley_ (EFSA Journal, 2011) and follow-up opinions; FDA qualified health claim (21 CFR 101.81). Verify current language. Glycemic index: standard GI tables (e.g. Foster-Powell, Holt & Brand-Miller). Celiac/hordein cross-reactivity: standard celiac disease guidelines (e.g. ACG). 麥芽 clinical dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. — clinical use should be verified against the current _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ and is not personalized medical advice.]

